From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hi3798 port <port@localhost>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 08:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: hisilicon: add the Hi3798MV200 IR receiver

drivers/media/rc/ir-hix5hd2.c has been in tree since 2014 and matches this
block, but hi3798mv200.dtsi never described it, so nothing probes.

The vendor device tree has it at 0xf8001000 on SPI 47. Its clock is already
in crg-hi3798mv200.c, in the sysctrl half rather than the CRG:

  { HI3798MV200_IR_CLK, "clk_ir", "clk_osc",
	  CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, 0x48, 4, 0, },

which is how hi3798cv200.dtsi wires up the same block. 0xf8001000 sits in the
always-on region next to sysctrl and gpio5 -- a remote has to be able to wake
a box that is off.

Left disabled; boards enable it and pick a keymap.
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/hisilicon/hi3798mv200.dtsi | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/hisilicon/hi3798mv200.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/hisilicon/hi3798mv200.dtsi
index e9d0d384c1bb..ebfa0506c4cc 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/hisilicon/hi3798mv200.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/hisilicon/hi3798mv200.dtsi
@@ -187,6 +216,14 @@ sysctrl: system-controller@8000000 {
 			#reset-cells = <2>;
 		};
 
+		ir: ir@8001000 {
+			compatible = "hisilicon,hix5hd2-ir";
+			reg = <0x8001000 0x1000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 47 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&sysctrl HI3798MV200_IR_CLK>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
 		gpio5: gpio@8004000 {
 			compatible = "arm,pl061", "arm,primecell";
 			reg = <0x8004000 0x1000>;
--
2.43.0

